      "result": "All 19 checklist items have been tested and recorded — the suite is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\n**15 PASS / 4 FAIL**\n\nThe core feature set is solid. Dashboard creation and editing, browsing, filtering, search, favorites, and the checklist system all work well. Search is genuinely thorough — it matches names, descriptions, locations, and vendor specialties case-insensitively, and results update live without a submit. Filters combine correctly, counts stay accurate, and there's a proper empty state. Persistence via localStorage is reliable across navigation and reload, including per-task completion.\n\n### Failures\n\n**IX-12 — Detail views are entirely unimplemented.** Every \"View Details\" and \"View Profile\" button is inert: no modal, no route change, no DOM mutation, no console error. This is the most significant gap, since a whole advertised capability (viewing complete listing details) is unreachable.\n\n**CS-8, CS-9, CS-11 — The wedding details form performs no validation whatsoever.** Every invalid value I tried was accepted and persisted over the previously saved data:\n- Budget `0` → displays `$0`; budget `-500` → displays `$-500`\n- Guests `-10` → displays `-10`; guests `12.5` → displays `12.5`\n- Date `2020-01-15` (past) → displays `Jan 15, 2020`, and the countdown element silently vanishes rather than erroring\n\nThese three share one root cause — the save handler commits raw input without any guards — so a single validation layer would close all of them.\n\n**IX-19 — Cancel doesn't discard the draft.** Worth flagging as subtler than it looks. Cancel *appears* to work because the dashboard correctly keeps its saved values. But the draft state isn't reset, so reopening the editor shows the abandoned edits (`DraftName`, `99999`, `500`). A user who cancels, reopens, and saves anything would silently persist edits they explicitly rejected. Combined with the missing validation above, a cancelled `-500` budget could later be committed without the user ever intending it.\n\n### Note on judgment calls\n\nFor IX-13 I accepted the aggregate dashboard progress of 10%, which the app computes over saved checklists only (1 of 10 tasks) rather than all 33 tasks. Both scopings are defensible readings of \"aggregate progress,\" and the value did update responsively in both directions, so I didn't treat the scoping choice as a defect. Similarly, CT-15's \"favorite counts **or** previews\" was satisfied by counts, and the app additionally provides previews.",
